# Version: 1.1.1 (2026-10-15)
# crontab.example
#
# The cron lines that wire up the four scripts in this archive. Copy the
# lines you want into `crontab -e`, or keep this file under version control
# and load it with `crontab crontab.example` (that REPLACES your whole
# crontab, so merge first if you already have entries).
#
# Keep a committed copy of your crontab. security-audit.sh compares the live
# crontab against that copy every run and reports drift. Point its
# CRONTAB_CANONICAL at the committed file.
#
# Three cron gotchas the book calls out:
#   1. cron does not read your shell profile. Set env vars here or inside
#      the scripts. PATH below is a safe default for Debian and Ubuntu.
#   2. Keep every entry on one line.
#   3. cron treats a bare % as a newline. Write any % in a command as \%.
#
# Times are host-local. Check `timedatectl` if you are not sure what that is.
#
# TODO: reader -- replace <INSTALL_DIR> with the absolute directory where you
#                 put these scripts, and <LOG_DIR> with a writable log directory.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
# Set MAILTO to an address if you want cron to email nonzero exits.
MAILTO=""

# Weekly disk cleanup, Sunday 02:00. Chapter 6.
0 2 * * 0 <INSTALL_DIR>/disk-cleanup.sh >> <LOG_DIR>/disk-cleanup.log 2>&1

# Daily disk-usage post, 16:00. Chapter 6.
0 16 * * * <INSTALL_DIR>/disk-usage-post.sh >> <LOG_DIR>/disk-usage-post.log 2>&1

# "Are you still there?" heartbeat check, every 6 hours. Chapter 6.
0 */6 * * * <INSTALL_DIR>/heartbeat-check.sh >> <LOG_DIR>/heartbeat-check.log 2>&1

# Security audit, every 4 hours. Chapter 11. Quiet when clean.
0 */4 * * * <INSTALL_DIR>/security-audit.sh >> <LOG_DIR>/security-audit.log 2>&1
